WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
The world is largely asleep.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.200
Digital paper money, or fiat as we know it today, is a big problem because they can just print it easily.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.000
And there is no way to audit the supply or control it.

4
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.440
So that is how money gets corrupted.

5
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.680
What Bitcoin does is it brings it back to the origin of money, which is there is no physical form to Bitcoin.

6
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.800
If you know 12 words, you have your money.

7
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.100
What this indicates to me is the entire fiat system is on its last legs.

8
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.340
These are the death throes of the fiat system.

9
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.660
It's almost over.

10
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.840
And I believe these things are more sudden than gradual.

11
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.100
I believe it's much sooner than that, simply because these transitions are very fast and very violent.

12
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.120
Very violent.

13
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.760
The amount of people that understand Bitcoin and can talk intelligently about Bitcoin is probably less than 1% of the population.

14
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.380
Then you have more percentages that kind of have it, are exposed to it.

15
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.600
But by and large, most people don't really understand.

16
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.360
And they don't even understand the legacy monetary system.

17
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.100
So you have a huge gap in understanding there.

18
00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.940
But reality kind of destroys that.

19
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.620
So when nobody wants fiat money, then nobody will want fiat money.

20
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.980
It'll be a very sharp drop off.

21
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:12.160
If you look at history, there's many examples in history of money just being worthless in a period of weeks and months.

22
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.400
There's the 10,000 limit for moving cash across borders.

23
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:21.260
But if you go back in time when that law was first passed, the real value of that money back then was like 80,000.

24
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.900
Because they're fixed on these numbers that are getting more worthless all the time.

25
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.760
And the reason it's getting more worthless is because, one, you're printing more of it.

26
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.840
But two, you're eliminating a key property of money, which is it has to free flow.

27
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.380
When a government has Bitcoin, who's holding the private key?

28
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.540
Well, that's an interesting one.

29
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.180
So there's a big push for green energy.

30
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.800
And green energy is not green.

31
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:51.300
It's sort of a scam to let the government pay companies that do specific things and subsidize them.

32
00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.780
And maybe money comes back and lines someone's pocket.

33
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:54.260
Who knows?

34
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.340
There is no energy shortage.

35
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.580
It's an intelligence shortage.

36
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.880
But there's always a reason behind the narrative.

37
00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:58.540
Follow the money.

38
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.620
Long term, Bitcoin will keep appreciating to a million a coin, to three million a coin, to ten million a coin.

39
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.800
Do you actually think it's going to go to one million?

40
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:06.380
Yeah.

41
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:07.760
How long does it take?

42
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:08.660
Within the next year.

43
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:09.440
Really?

44
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.140
A mil?

45
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:10.580
Yeah.

46
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:11.340
A Bitcoin?

47
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:12.880
In the future, let's say in 20 years.

48
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.440
How many Bitcoin do you need to be seen as doing okay?

49
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.500
I believe there's a small group of people that know who Satoshi is.

50
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:58.520
How many Bitcoin do you need to be seen as doing okay?

51
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.300
We're going to talk about Bitcoin today.

52
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.380
Really exciting stuff.

53
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.780
But for people that are unfamiliar, what's your journey like into Bitcoin?

54
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.540
When I first started getting interested in Bitcoin, it was 2011 or 2013.

55
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.900
So you're just kind of reading about it through the news.

56
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.660
And most of it's very negative.

57
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.040
Right.

58
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.460
And then I think I caught one article in 2013, which was talking about Bitcoin mining.

59
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.000
So prior to reading that one, I just assumed it was some sort of digital money that someone made.

60
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.820
But then when I read the mining article, I decided to follow the guide to set up your own mining operation.

61
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.560
I tried to set it up on my laptop.

62
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.860
And it's too late to mine on a laptop then.

63
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.140
But going through that whole process showed me that there is actually nobody controlling the network.

64
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:41.520
Right.

65
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.640
So the supply is produced by these nodes on the network, the mining nodes.

66
00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.640
And that was when it kind of clicked for me that this is a unique innovation.

67
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.220
But I was still very busy with work.

68
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.960
So I didn't have time to get into the industry until late 2014, early 2015 when I joined BTC China.

69
00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.740
How do you get from there to BTC China?

70
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.640
My friend Bobby was the CEO at the time.

71
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:05.920
Right.

72
00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.720
We're in Shanghai near the Shanghai Stadium.

73
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.420
So he's on the one side.

74
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.240
I was on the other side.

75
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.000
So he asked me to be an advisor first to build the mobile team.

76
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.100
So I would just go over in the afternoon a couple of days a week and help him out.

77
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.360
And then I was formerly an advisor and then I was appointed COO.

78
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:34.380
From an article or one of your interviews, you said the tokenomics of Bitcoin and game tokens are similar and it clicks for you.

79
00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.500
They're both digital assets.

80
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:41.240
I was interested in digital assets because I came from that world, both playing and building in it.

81
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:49.920
But Bitcoin kind of showed me that there's another way because when you have a game economy, you're pretty much anchored to the company.

82
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.560
You're anchored to the monetary policy and everything from the developer.

83
00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.520
Game economies, it's the game developer operator that decides what's the inflation rate.

84
00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.060
Having built these online games, I've had to manage that myself too.

85
00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:12.260
So if the money supply is increasing too much, you need to create more sinks and create reasons for players to spend their money.

86
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.340
Otherwise, you get hyperinflation.

87
00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.560
And it's the same with the quote unquote real economy with the Fed.

88
00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.820
They raise rates to try to rein things in and they try to manage it.

89
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.440
But I think Bitcoin is different because there is no one managing it.

90
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.060
You have a protocol that's based off of math and that's it.

91
00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.920
There's no negotiation with Bitcoin.

92
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.920
If the price is low, not much you can do.

93
00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:38.820
Price is high.

94
00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:39.680
That's it.

95
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:40.060
Yeah.

96
00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.940
Inflation and interest rate.

97
00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.260
The government is actually encouraging you to spend.

98
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.280
Otherwise, I would just put my cash under my bed.

99
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:47.560
Right.

100
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.480
Because I expect things to be more expensive in the future.

101
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.300
And it's good for the economy.

102
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.480
So what about Bitcoin?

103
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.040
Like people see this fiat system and say that is broken.

104
00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.100
But is it really broken?

105
00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.340
I would say so.

106
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.420
So fiat is an abomination.

107
00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.540
It should not exist.

108
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.520
If you go to the origin of money, it's peer to peer.

109
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.320
It is just people doing things and trading with one another.

110
00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.600
Money is a layer for trade.

111
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:25.620
So if I specialize in making hats and you specialize in growing apples, we can trade one hat for three apples or something like that.

112
00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:32.920
But if I don't need an apple or you don't want a hat, then I can owe you and I can still take the apple from you.

113
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:33.220
Right.

114
00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:35.440
So money is really a layer for us to trade.

115
00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:43.380
And then when you network that over the planet with all humanity, you kind of have this invisible layer of money.

116
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:54.060
And that's anchored to the physical realm historically through things like seashells, salt, gold, and paper.

117
00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:59.120
And now digitized paper, but controlled by centralized parties.

118
00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.880
But the origin of money is it's just an invisible ledger.

119
00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:13.240
And when you try to bring it down to the real world and give it a physical property or physical attribute, that's an attack vector.

120
00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.920
It's a weak point because someone can now control the ledger.

121
00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:17.440
Right.

122
00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:24.240
So if you have gold as your money, wherever you put your gold, that's an attack vector.

123
00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.600
They can switch it out, lend it, and just fill the vault with lead, painted gold.

124
00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:29.860
Yeah.

125
00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:35.260
If it's paper money, well, actually paper money is kind of okay if you recognize it, but you have counterfeiting.

126
00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.960
But digital paper money or fiat as we know it today is a big problem because they can just print it easily.

127
00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.300
And there is no way to audit the supply or control it.

128
00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.220
So that is how money gets corrupted.

129
00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:53.540
What Bitcoin does is it brings it back to the origin of money, which is there is no physical form to Bitcoin.

130
00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.080
If you know 12 words, you have your money.

131
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:05.740
So I think this is a restoration of money back to what it must be, which is dematerialized and singular in purpose, which is tracking value transfer.

132
00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:15.560
I read this article that MIT's listed down like 10 top tech of 2024, and one of them is quant computing.

133
00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:24.720
And apparently, quant computing would be able to break the encryption system in years.

134
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:30.140
It is theoretically possible, but we're nowhere near that happening.

135
00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.180
Quantum computing could potentially attack Bitcoin, right?

136
00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:37.300
One is public-private key generation, guessing your private key.

137
00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:45.680
The other would be mining, surpassing the Bitcoin mining network now and getting the nonce and getting the blocks.

138
00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.600
So let's break those two things apart.

139
00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.860
So the first one is the breaking of basic public-private key encryption.

140
00:08:52.860 --> 00:09:04.480
When that happens, I think there are bigger problems because that will stop general e-commerce around the world and even more dangerous things like nuclear launch codes and the like.

141
00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:11.560
So I think there are more problems to be solved as a whole from humanity than let's just worry about quantum computing killing Bitcoin.

142
00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:12.840
The other part is the mining.

143
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.780
If quantum computing does become a real thing, it's not there yet.

144
00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:27.360
But I still am not sure it would be able to compete with mining as we have it today with ASICs because you'll have one quantum computer when it emerges.

145
00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.640
It'll be very expensive and very difficult to maintain.

146
00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:39.980
And you'll have competition with millions and millions of ASICs that are all working in parallel.

147
00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:48.240
One quantum computer is not likely able to compete with all of the Bitcoin hashing power because you can just add more and more.

148
00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:52.960
But I don't think we're at a stage yet where you can simply build another quantum computer.

149
00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.080
They're not even able to get really one kind of really working.

150
00:09:58.440 --> 00:09:59.740
So it's more like a concept.

151
00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:00.900
I would think so.

152
00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.200
But the media loves to.

153
00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.080
Yeah, because it got me really worried.

154
00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:04.900
I'm like, what?

155
00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:06.560
Well, it's just bad reporting.

156
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.200
Some reporter says, what are Bitcoin threats?

157
00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.640
And then hypothetically, quantum computing.

158
00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.800
And then they run with it without doing any actual research into the state of quantum computing.

159
00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.220
And they put down like 2025 or something.

160
00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:20.820
And I'm like, wow, that's so soon.

161
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:24.120
Well, we should have been in an ice age in 1980.

162
00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:24.980
Right.

163
00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.540
So that is true.

164
00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:27.340
That is true.

165
00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.360
You're the CEO of Gen3.

166
00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:30.740
Can you tell us what do you do?

167
00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:32.660
Gen3 is a Bitcoin tech company.

168
00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.600
And our focus is on accelerating Bitcoin adoption around the world.

169
00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:42.440
So to do that, we work with governments, politicians at the top down level.

170
00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:47.500
And at the bottom up level, we have our Aqua Wallet, which is a Bitcoin in layer two wallet,

171
00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:52.100
which is meant to get grassroots adoption and help spin up new circular economies.

172
00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.160
So things like Bitcoin Beach, Bitcoin Jungle in other places around the world,

173
00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.260
they can use the wallet to onboard people to the Bitcoin network.

174
00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.620
Can you explain more on Bitcoin Jungle and Bitcoin Beach?

175
00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.620
So these are little communities that started out just using Bitcoin.

176
00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:07.000
Right.

177
00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.000
Because of necessity.

178
00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:12.020
So in Costa Rica, if you're a foreigner, you cannot get a local bank account.

179
00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:17.360
So they just started using Bitcoin to pay local merchants and local merchants started accepting it.

180
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.520
Now there's an integration with the banking system through SinPay.

181
00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:27.380
So they can kind of onboard people to take Bitcoin and they can convert it to the local money.

182
00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.480
These local economies that are Bitcoin fueled.

183
00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:34.280
You can go and live off Bitcoin in all these tiny little economies.

184
00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.980
So let's say I buy a sandwich every day and it's $5.

185
00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.140
But if I pay in Bitcoin, how do I calculate the value?

186
00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:47.300
It'll be using the price feed of whatever wallet you're paying with.

187
00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:51.620
So they'll price it in pesos or in whatever.

188
00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.320
And you'll pay in Bitcoin, but denominated in fiat.

189
00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.420
Is that how you see the world or do you see the world in Bitcoin standard?

190
00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.620
The world is running on a dollar standard right now.

191
00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:02.840
Right.

192
00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.300
But what we're going to is a Bitcoin standardized world.

193
00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:10.700
But there's probably five to 10 years before we hit that point.

194
00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:15.820
So our slogan is we are accelerating hyper-Bitcoinization at Jan 3.

195
00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:20.000
So that's the point at which you no longer convert Bitcoin to fiat money.

196
00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.480
And people don't want fiat money at that point.

197
00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:23.380
They only want Bitcoin.

198
00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.660
So they won't even accept dollars.

199
00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:30.020
The point of hyper-Bitcoinization and hyperinflation are sort of one in the same.

200
00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:35.720
It just becomes a transition to Bitcoin and you don't want the dollars anymore.

201
00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.480
In Nigeria, I think sometime last year, they were switching currency.

202
00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.720
Like they had an old currency and they want to remove it.

203
00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.640
And people are trying to get USD instead, but they couldn't because there's a shortage.

204
00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:52.100
So do you think Bitcoin adoption is easier and weaker economy?

205
00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:53.600
Because there's an incentive.

206
00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:54.960
Well, there's a necessity.

207
00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:55.640
Right.

208
00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:56.660
So they need Bitcoin.

209
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.660
And that was why in Africa you see many regions with a premium on Bitcoin.

210
00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.260
And it just has to do with government controls, right?

211
00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.800
They don't want you to have access to anything else.

212
00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.560
They want you to use their local currency.

213
00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:15.380
And it was the same situation in Argentina where the bank had one rate and then you have the blue rate.

214
00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:17.400
The street real rate, right?

215
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.440
So, you know, nobody really recognizes the bank rate.

216
00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:21.740
It's always the market rate.

217
00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:22.020
Right.

218
00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.440
And the market always wins.

219
00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:24.420
You can't fight the market.

220
00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.720
And we're seeing that with the Japanese yen.

221
00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.760
They're fighting the market and they're not successful.

222
00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:35.620
But what this indicates to me is the entire fiat system is on its last legs.

223
00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.320
These are the death throes of the fiat system.

224
00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:39.900
It's almost over.

225
00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.860
And I believe these things are more sudden than gradual.

226
00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:46.880
I believe it's much sooner than that.

227
00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:53.560
Simply because these transitions are very fast and very violent.

228
00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:54.820
Very violent.

229
00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.340
Help me visualize.

230
00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:57.520
Like five years.

231
00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.540
It's because the world is largely asleep.

232
00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:01.240
Right.

233
00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:09.420
Like the amount of people that understand Bitcoin and can talk intelligently about Bitcoin is probably less than, I don't know, 1% of the population.

234
00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:13.740
Then you have more percentages that kind of have it, are exposed to it.

235
00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.400
But by and large, most people don't really understand.

236
00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:19.700
And they don't even understand the legacy monetary system.

237
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.980
So you have a huge gap in understanding there.

