00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:52.620
Yeah.

726
00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:53.080
Right?

727
00:40:53.540 --> 00:40:59.120
That's like a good amount of money for someone back when dollars were worth something, right?

728
00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:02.620
You have $10,000 that buys you a decent amount of stuff.

729
00:41:02.820 --> 00:41:03.420
A house.

730
00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:05.160
Not right now.

731
00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:06.440
No, not right now.

732
00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:06.740
No.

733
00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:09.640
But that's why the money is ending, right?

734
00:41:09.740 --> 00:41:15.960
If you look at the past 100 years from 1924 to now, the dollar has basically gone to zero.

735
00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:21.680
So it is happening, but no one is really seeing it.

736
00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:26.040
It's just the problem of the frog in the pot.

737
00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:28.180
It's boiling, but it's boiling slowly.

738
00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:30.260
So the frog doesn't want to jump out.

739
00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:35.500
But when you're in Bitcoin, you see the pot because you're outside of that situation.

740
00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:37.520
And you see the frog in the pot.

741
00:41:37.780 --> 00:41:39.380
So then you understand what's happening.

742
00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:44.720
And I think it's our responsibility to try to educate the world about the state of the system

743
00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:46.640
and where it's going to end up inevitably.

744
00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:51.260
What is your view on any other crypto except for Bitcoin?

745
00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:52.980
I think it's all a distraction.

746
00:41:53.660 --> 00:41:53.820
Right.

747
00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:58.040
So Bitcoin is the only cryptocurrency that you recognize.

748
00:41:58.600 --> 00:41:58.700
Yeah.

749
00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:04.700
So I was at one of the events here last night and someone asked me and I just told, they said,

750
00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:06.280
what is the difference between Bitcoin and crypto?

751
00:42:06.600 --> 00:42:09.800
And the difference is anybody can make a crypto project.

752
00:42:10.340 --> 00:42:10.900
You can do one.

753
00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:11.240
Yes.

754
00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:11.660
Yes.

755
00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:12.800
Anybody can make one.

756
00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:14.120
Nobody can make Bitcoin again.

757
00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:15.160
Here's the question.

758
00:42:15.380 --> 00:42:19.120
So I believe there's a small group of people that know who Satoshi is.

759
00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:20.340
I don't think so.

760
00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:21.500
You don't think so?

761
00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:21.820
No.

762
00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:23.080
How is it possible?

763
00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:25.940
Because he was really good at hiding his identity.

764
00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:27.900
Are you Satoshi?

765
00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:28.400
No, I'm not.

766
00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:29.720
Because he's pointing at you.

767
00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:31.280
I'm not.

768
00:42:31.400 --> 00:42:31.540
I'm not.

769
00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:35.420
So you don't believe there's someone that know the real identity?

770
00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:36.580
I don't think so.

771
00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:37.660
People will guess.

772
00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:38.120
Yeah.

773
00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:41.400
I don't think anyone can find him through any means.

774
00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:43.180
He was very clever at hiding his box.

775
00:42:43.600 --> 00:42:48.520
But, you know, recently or not recently, a few months ago, there's like reveal of emails

776
00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:49.780
and all that, you know.

777
00:42:50.540 --> 00:42:56.220
You don't think there'll be a day where people actually find out who that person is?

778
00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:56.760
I don't think so.

779
00:42:56.760 --> 00:43:00.560
How can one person in this world just hide?

780
00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:03.700
He was very good at what he did.

781
00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:05.620
He didn't leak his IP address.

782
00:43:05.780 --> 00:43:08.540
When he bought a domain, it's not trackable.

783
00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:10.280
I think he paid with Bitcoin or something.

784
00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:12.780
But like there is no path back.

785
00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:14.140
And now he disappeared.

786
00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:14.720
Yeah.

787
00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:15.700
It's even harder.

788
00:43:15.700 --> 00:43:19.100
I think he was rock solid in his OPSEC.

789
00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:19.720
Mm hmm.

790
00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:28.400
Hypothetically, in theory, if a person actually reveal the identity of Satoshi, is it going

791
00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:29.920
to be devastating for Bitcoin?

792
00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:33.060
I don't think anyone can prove he is.

793
00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:35.580
Not with the amount of evidence that we've managed to collect.

794
00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:37.320
So there's no way to prove it.

795
00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:43.440
He could come out and prove it himself by using a key, signing something.

796
00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:45.280
But I don't think so.

797
00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:49.080
If he wanted to come back, he would have come back, you know, when Bitcoin was

798
00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:52.140
worth something because when he left, it was still not very valuable, right?

799
00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:58.560
So if he can hold out when Bitcoin was worth, I don't know, a billion dollars, he can definitely

800
00:43:58.560 --> 00:43:59.340
hold out now.

801
00:43:59.860 --> 00:44:04.760
Or he's destroyed the ability to move coins or sign for transactions, too.

802
00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:07.520
Maybe he knew there will be a temptation in the future.

803
00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:09.400
So he just deleted everything.

804
00:44:09.620 --> 00:44:12.020
What do you think the mission is for him?

805
00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:13.080
It's hard to say.

806
00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:17.160
I mean, I don't like to guess, but I think it has to do with fixing the money.

807
00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:22.320
If you look at the newspaper headline, if you look at some of the things that he seems

808
00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:26.360
interested in, like eCash, they're all ways to protect people.

809
00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:28.120
It's a very cypherpunk ethos.

810
00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:36.860
It's about sovereignty, permissionlessness, writing code, and fixing the world.

811
00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:39.460
What is your reason of being Bitcoin?

812
00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:41.100
It's fixing the world.

813
00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:49.900
So you got attracted or you got into Bitcoin because of this decentralization idea or?

814
00:44:50.380 --> 00:44:53.300
Well, decentralization is a means to an end.

815
00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:54.040
Right.

816
00:44:54.040 --> 00:44:57.300
The end that I think a lot of Bitcoiners want is to fix the world.

817
00:44:57.420 --> 00:45:02.320
So it's a better place where you don't have a 1984 scenario, right?

818
00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:07.300
That we're not tracked like cattle and everything you do and say is monitored.

819
00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:11.460
There's really no counter force to that aside from Bitcoin, right?

820
00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:15.120
Because technology tends towards centralization.

821
00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:16.840
It always is that way.

822
00:45:17.940 --> 00:45:22.320
Once you get there, it's hard to break out of it unless there's a very powerful counter force,

823
00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:24.720
which is Bitcoin, which is completely decentralized.

824
00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:28.080
So without Bitcoin, there's really no hope.

825
00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:34.360
That's why I'm pushing so hard to get Bitcoin adoption and awareness about Bitcoin.

826
00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:37.380
Has your thought changed over the years?

827
00:45:38.060 --> 00:45:39.960
Let's say Bitcoin hit all time high.

828
00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:41.540
You're like, oh, I have all this Bitcoin.

829
00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:44.740
I could change that to a yacht or like a mansion.

830
00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:48.040
I think all that stuff is very distracting.

831
00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:52.320
Like it's more maintenance, more work than it's worth.

832
00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:54.260
It's better just to rent it, right?

833
00:45:54.460 --> 00:45:54.760
Okay.

834
00:45:55.160 --> 00:45:59.840
But I think some people like to show off and get a fancy car or a fancy boat.

835
00:46:01.040 --> 00:46:03.700
Oh, do you think in the future we will own nothing?

836
00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:05.120
And just rent?

837
00:46:05.520 --> 00:46:07.260
No, I think people will own things.

838
00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:08.040
Right.

839
00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:10.680
I mean, that's the WEF thing, right?

840
00:46:10.700 --> 00:46:11.120
Yeah, yeah.

841
00:46:11.140 --> 00:46:11.980
Nothing to be happy.

842
00:46:12.220 --> 00:46:16.340
No, I think if anything, we will have better property rights.

843
00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:22.540
Because I think with Bitcoin, it teaches people again why property rights are important.

844
00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:27.240
Because you understand that owning something is important, right?

845
00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:30.800
And aside from Bitcoin, you can't really own anything else.

846
00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:37.580
So you learn that the entire system of property rights and laws to protect property rights is actually very weak.

847
00:46:38.040 --> 00:46:40.700
Like again, in Canada, your bank account can be frozen.

848
00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:44.440
If you own real estate, it can still be seized.

849
00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:50.360
And there's an eminent domain or appropriation.

850
00:46:50.780 --> 00:46:54.120
So if the government wants your land, they can technically force you to sell it.

851
00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:58.780
So I think if anything, Bitcoin will increase property rights across the board.

852
00:46:58.780 --> 00:47:09.760
It probably won't happen in the next five years, but in 20, 30 years, we should see the respect of property rights permeate through the legacy system.

853
00:47:10.100 --> 00:47:13.900
You're trying to push Bitcoin adoption through the government.

854
00:47:13.900 --> 00:47:17.160
But the end game is everyone using Bitcoin.

855
00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:21.300
How do you actually get everyone on board?

856
00:47:21.480 --> 00:47:22.340
Is it a wallet?

857
00:47:23.360 --> 00:47:26.040
There are countries where people don't have a smartphone yet.

858
00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:29.600
So how exactly is that going to happen?

859
00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:30.780
That's why we're building Aqua.

860
00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:36.580
So that's another piece of the puzzle, which is creating a very accessible wallet that anybody can use.

861
00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:39.020
That we can hide a lot of the complexity.

862
00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:42.000
So if you want to just send Bitcoin, it just works.

863
00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:46.880
There's no complexity with things like channel management.

864
00:47:46.880 --> 00:47:50.300
If you're using Lightning Network, you don't need to set up a Lightning node.

865
00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:52.940
We can do swaps to pay Lightning invoices.

866
00:47:54.520 --> 00:48:00.840
We're incorporating Tether into Aqua as well, because we see that as a vehicle to onboard a lot of people to Bitcoin.

867
00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:07.880
So in the global south, most of them, most people there are trying to protect their wealth, their savings.

868
00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:10.940
And they're doing that by using USDT.

869
00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:15.480
And maybe there's a hundred million people doing that.

870
00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:18.440
But they're not into Bitcoin because they don't understand that.

871
00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:19.800
But they did seek out Tether.

872
00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:26.500
And they are using Tether for everyday purchases, you know, just living life.

873
00:48:26.660 --> 00:48:28.620
So it's become their digital dollar.

874
00:48:29.100 --> 00:48:33.040
And I think what we can do with Aqua is bridge them into Bitcoin.

875
00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:38.020
Because most of the wallets that are powering stable coins, they have their own altcoin chain, right?

876
00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:40.000
Either Ethereum, Tron or whatever.

877
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:42.040
But with Aqua, it's all Bitcoin.

878
00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:43.200
It's Bitcoin and Liquid.

879
00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:45.300
So it's a Bitcoin and a sidechain.

880
00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:49.400
So we can get them into the Bitcoin ecosystem that way.

881
00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:50.500
At least that's the hope.

882
00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:52.780
Does it look like an app?

883
00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:58.880
Like I'm trying to visualize how to get everybody, like my grandma who's like 80, to be on board.

884
00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:04.520
Like is it going to look like an app that you scan or Apple Pay?

885
00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:04.920
Yeah.

886
00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:09.220
Well, it'll be using QR codes or lightning addresses.

887
00:49:09.700 --> 00:49:16.000
But you'll be able to just ask your grandma for her lightning address and send Bitcoin to her that way.

888
00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:18.660
Does that take a lot of education though?

889
00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:22.080
Well, I think there's still a lot of UI work to be done.

890
00:49:22.080 --> 00:49:27.240
But we can make it far smoother and simpler for the mass market.

891
00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:32.560
Because right now, I think most Bitcoin wallets don't really seem to be targeting the next billion people.

892
00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:38.580
They're building something for people that are already in Bitcoin and are already advanced users.

893
00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:42.900
Whereas for us, we want to make it so easy for people to use this.

894
00:49:43.280 --> 00:49:46.620
It just makes sense and they understand it and it just works.

895
00:49:47.460 --> 00:49:51.120
When Bitcoin Spot ETF came out, I had mixed feelings.

896
00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:56.980
On one hand, it's getting the recognition and it's a new asset class now.

897
00:49:57.520 --> 00:50:06.380
And on the other hand, it doesn't seem like big companies or issuers owning that much Bitcoin is decentralized.

898
00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:08.540
So I want to know your thought.

899
00:50:08.820 --> 00:50:14.160
Is this just a process or a step closer to everyone having Bitcoin?

900
00:50:14.300 --> 00:50:17.460
But then all the Bitcoin is with them.

901
00:50:17.620 --> 00:50:18.960
Well, it doesn't really matter.

902
00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:21.660
So Bitcoin is based on proof of work.

903
00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:22.140
Right.

904
00:50:22.200 --> 00:50:23.500
Which means you have to mine it.

905
00:50:23.820 --> 00:50:23.960
Yeah.

906
00:50:24.920 --> 00:50:26.820
Ethereum, I think, is in danger with the ETS.

907
00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:33.960
Because if it gets centralized with a custodian, then effectively they control it because it's proof of stake.

908
00:50:34.060 --> 00:50:34.220
Right.

909
00:50:34.220 --> 00:50:40.700
So if you have the biggest stake by default through proxy, through investors, then you control the network.

910
00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:43.680
And they can change the rules of the network that way.

911
00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:43.920
Right.

912
00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:46.740
But they can't really.

913
00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:50.140
With Bitcoin, you can't because you can just have it.

914
00:50:50.140 --> 00:50:54.380
But having it doesn't impact the network itself.

915
00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:59.420
Like being able to transact it, the rules of the network and mining of the asset.

916
00:50:59.640 --> 00:50:59.740
Right.

917
00:50:59.960 --> 00:51:01.120
It's not connected at all.

918
00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:01.520
Right.

919
00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:03.960
So with proof of stake systems, the rich get richer.

920
00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:06.900
It's actually a direct parallel to the fiat system.

921
00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:08.740
If you're rich, you get richer.

922
00:51:09.320 --> 00:51:11.140
With Bitcoin, you don't.

923
00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:13.080
Having Bitcoin doesn't make you richer.

924
00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:18.080
Bitcoin appreciating against other things does make you richer.

925
00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:19.020
But that's all relative.

926
00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:19.340
Right.

927
00:51:19.520 --> 00:51:21.520
But having more does not make more.

928
00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:25.320
So you don't have that snowball effect where the rich keep getting richer over time.

929
00:51:25.400 --> 00:51:25.740
I see.

930
00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:29.500
Whereas with proof of stake systems, that is exactly what you get.

931
00:51:29.620 --> 00:51:34.160
You get the same thing now, but with a blockchain and with Vitalik.

932
00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:37.720
There are people in this world that are so rich.

933
00:51:38.260 --> 00:51:41.280
They're richer than a lot of countries already.

934
00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:44.600
But they're just, you know, people don't know who they are.

935
00:51:44.660 --> 00:51:44.820
Yeah.

936
00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:47.640
And those are people that hold a lot of Bitcoin.

937
00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:52.840
And at one point, they'll be so rich, they can have their own army.

938
00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:59.900
And it just sounds like we're going back to like where, you know, you have your own castle,

939
00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:03.180
your own army, and you're richer than a country.

940
00:52:03.500 --> 00:52:07.500
So I mean, micro strategy is richer than many countries in Bitcoin too.

941
00:52:07.700 --> 00:52:07.840
Yeah.

942
00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:12.840
So if Bitcoin does go to a million or 10 million, you'll have that case where a corporation

943
00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:14.720
is wealthier than many countries.

944
00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:18.300
And you think that's happening now.

945
00:52:18.820 --> 00:52:21.480
Well, there's no way to counter that.

946
00:52:21.520 --> 00:52:22.100
Like, what do you do?

947
00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:24.680
Then you go into collectivism, right?

948
00:52:24.740 --> 00:52:26.160
Like, okay, you have too much Bitcoin.

949
00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:29.580
We're going to take some from you and divide it up amongst everyone else.

950
00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:31.100
Just for equity, right?

951
00:52:31.460 --> 00:52:34.740
But I think it's just an inevitable end state.

952
00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:39.600
You will always have concentration of wealth and people that had foresight.

953
00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:40.500
Right, right.

954
00:52:40.580 --> 00:52:44.100
And I don't think there's that much you can do about it.

955
00:52:44.460 --> 00:52:50.740
If you're a 30-year-old that's watching this show, how much Bitcoin do you think that person

956
00:52:50.740 --> 00:52:52.880
should have to retire comfortably?

957
00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:53.740
I don't know.

958
00:52:53.740 --> 00:52:57.260
I think you should not think about how much Bitcoin you should have to retire.

959
00:52:57.260 --> 00:53:03.020
You should find ways to create value where you can have a steady stream of income, which

960
00:53:03.020 --> 00:53:04.360
you can allocate to Bitcoin.

961
00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:05.400
Right.

962
00:53:05.720 --> 00:53:05.980
Right.

963
00:53:06.140 --> 00:53:06.340
Right.

964
00:53:06.420 --> 00:53:06.640
Okay.

965
00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:07.600
So let's say...

966
00:53:07.600 --> 00:53:11.520
If you want to get rich and just get Bitcoin and never do anything else, I don't think that

967
00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:12.180
will end well.

968
00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:17.920
It's better to have a fiat mining operation that can convert to Bitcoin.

969
00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:18.120
Bitcoin.

